<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-7-7-arraylist" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Dynamic Arrays: The ArrayList Class &amp; Wrapper Types</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      You’ve now seen the main drawback of standard arrays: once an array is created, its size is locked in. If you underestimate how much space you need, you run out of room; if you overestimate, you waste memory. And as we saw with <c>removeValue</c>, inserting or deleting elements in the middle means shifting values around by hand.
    </p>

    <p>
      Still, standard arrays aren't going anywhere<mdash />when you know your data size up front, they have real advantages:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <title>Speed and memory efficiency</title>
        <p>
          Arrays store elements side by side in a single block of memory, making element access (<c>scores[i]</c>) practically instantaneous with very little memory overhead.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Built-in syntax</title>
        <p>
          Java gives arrays special <q>syntactic sugar,</q> including square-bracket indexing and <c>{ ... }</c> initializer lists that make them simple to read and write.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Native library support</title>
        <p>
          High-performance graphics, audio, and scientific libraries written in C and C++ can work directly with Java arrays.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      When you do need a collection that can grow and shrink as your program runs, though, Java gives you a better tool: the <c>ArrayList</c> class. An <c>ArrayList</c> is a dynamic array<mdash />it uses a partially filled array behind the scenes, automatically resizing and shifting elements when you add or remove items. Whenever your data's size isn't fixed in advance, reaching for <c>ArrayList</c> saves you from reinventing that machinery yourself (and risking subtle bugs and inefficiencies).
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-7-7-1-introducing-arraylist">
    <title>Introducing ArrayList</title>

    <p>
      To use <c>ArrayList</c> in a program, you must first import it from the <c>java.util</c> package:
    </p>

    <program language="java"><![CDATA[import java.util.ArrayList;]]></program>

    <p>
      Unlike a standard array, where the element type comes before square brackets (<c>String[]</c>), an <c>ArrayList</c> uses generics<mdash />a Java feature that lets a class act as a container for a type you specify inside angle brackets (<c>&lt; &gt;</c>). You can think of the type in angle brackets as a parameter that tells the compiler what kind of object the list is allowed to hold:
    </p>

    <program language="java"><![CDATA[ArrayList<String> roster = new ArrayList<String>();]]></program>

    <p>
      Because the compiler already knows the element type from the left side of the declaration, Java also lets you omit the repeated type inside the right-hand brackets, which is known as the diamond operator (<c>&lt;&gt;</c>):
    </p>

    <program language="java"><![CDATA[ArrayList<String> roster = new ArrayList<>();]]></program>

    <p>
      Both forms give you the same compile-time type safety: Java ensures that only <c>String</c> objects can be placed in <c>roster</c>, and it knows that anything retrieved from <c>roster</c> is a <c>String</c>. Notice also that you do not specify a capacity inside the parentheses when creating a default <c>ArrayList</c>. Behind the scenes, Java starts with an internal array of default capacity (10) and expands it automatically whenever you run out of space.
    </p>

    <p>
      To append an element to the end of an <c>ArrayList</c>, call the <c>add</c> method. Unlike a standard array<mdash />which uses the <c>.length</c> field to report its fixed capacity<mdash />an <c>ArrayList</c> uses the <c>size()</c> method to report how many elements are currently stored in the list:
    </p>

    <program language="java"><![CDATA[ArrayList<String> roster = new ArrayList<>();
System.out.println(roster.size());   // Prints 0

roster.add("Quanah");
roster.add("Devon");
roster.add("Priya");

System.out.println(roster.size());   // Prints 3
System.out.println(roster);          // Prints [Quanah, Devon, Priya]]]></program>

    <p>
      Notice two convenient differences from standard arrays:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <c>size()</c> tracks only the elements you have added (just like the <c>count</c> variable we maintained manually in Section 7.6), not the internal array's capacity.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Printing an <c>ArrayList</c> directly with <c>System.out.println</c> produces a readable list such as <c>[Quanah, Devon, Priya]</c> without needing <c>Arrays.toString()</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      To read or replace an element at a specific index (still zero-based, from <c>0</c> to <c>size() - 1</c>), use <c>get(index)</c> and <c>set(index, value)</c> instead of square brackets:
    </p>

    <program language="java"><![CDATA[String first = roster.get(0);        // "Quanah"
roster.set(1, "Mishki");             // Replaces "Devon" with "Mishki"]]></program>

    <p>
      <c>ArrayList</c> also automates the element-shifting operations we wrote by hand in Section 7.6:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <title>Inserting at an index</title>
        <p>
          Calling <c>add(index, element)</c> inserts the new element at <c>index</c> and shifts the existing element at <c>index</c> (and everything after it) one position to the right, increasing <c>size()</c> by 1. The index must be between <c>0</c> and <c>size()</c>, inclusive.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Removing by index</title>
        <p>
          Calling <c>remove(index)</c> removes and returns the element at <c>index</c>, shifting all subsequent elements one position to the left and decreasing <c>size()</c> by 1.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Removing by value</title>
        <p>
          Calling <c>remove(object)</c> searches for the first matching element (using <c>.equals()</c>) and removes it if found, returning <c>true</c> if the list changed and <c>false</c> otherwise.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <program language="java"><![CDATA[// roster is currently [Quanah, Mishki, Priya]
roster.add(1, "Elena");              // [Quanah, Elena, Mishki, Priya]
roster.remove(2);                    // Removes "Mishki" -> [Quanah, Elena, Priya]
roster.remove("Quanah");             // Removes "Quanah" -> [Elena, Priya]]]></program>

    <p>
      Just like standard arrays, accessing an invalid index (less than <c>0</c> or greater than or equal to <c>size()</c>) with <c>get</c>, <c>set</c>, or <c>remove</c> throws an <c>IndexOutOfBoundsException</c>. <xref ref="table-arraylist-methods" /> is a quick reference of <c>ArrayList</c> methods.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-arraylist-methods">
      <title>Quick reference of <c>ArrayList</c> methods</title>
      <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col width="25%"/>
        <col width="40%"/>
        <col width="35%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>ArrayList Method</cell>
          <cell>What It Does</cell>
          <cell>Standard Array Equivalent</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>list.size()</c></cell>
          <cell>Returns the number of elements currently stored in the list.</cell>
          <cell><c>count</c> (tracked manually; <c>arr.length</c> is fixed capacity)</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>list.add(item)</c></cell>
          <cell>Appends <c>item</c> to the end of the list.</cell>
          <cell><c>arr[count] = item; count++;</c> (if <c>count &lt; arr.length</c>)</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>list.add(index, item)</c></cell>
          <cell>Inserts <c>item</c> at <c>index</c>, shifting existing elements right.</cell>
          <cell>Manual right-shift loop + insert + <c>count++;</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>list.get(index)</c></cell>
          <cell>Returns the element at <c>index</c>.</cell>
          <cell><c>arr[index]</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>list.set(index, item)</c></cell>
          <cell>Replaces the element at <c>index</c> with <c>item</c> and returns the previous value.</cell>
          <cell><c>arr[index] = item;</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>list.remove(index)</c></cell>
          <cell>Removes and returns the element at <c>index</c>, shifting subsequent elements left.</cell>
          <cell>Manual left-shift loop + <c>count--;</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>list.remove(item)</c></cell>
          <cell>Removes the first matching element (via <c>.equals()</c>), returning <c>true</c> if found.</cell>
          <cell>Manual search + left-shift loop + <c>count--;</c></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-7-7-2-wrapper-classes">
    <title>Wrapper Classes &amp; Autoboxing/Unboxing</title>

    <p>
      Unlike standard arrays, an <c>ArrayList</c> cannot store primitive values such as <c>int</c>, <c>double</c>, or <c>boolean</c>. <c>ArrayList</c> is a generic collection that only stores references to objects on the heap. Attempting to declare an <c>ArrayList</c> of <c>int</c> (with <c>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</c>) will produce a compiler error.
    </p>

    <p>
      To bridge this gap, Java provides a wrapper class in the <c>java.lang</c> package for each of the eight primitive types. A wrapper object simply <q>wraps</q> a single primitive value inside an immutable object, allowing primitive data to be stored in collections like <c>ArrayList</c>. Wrapper classes also provide useful constants and utility methods you have already encountered, such as <c>Integer.MAX_VALUE</c>, <c>Integer.parseInt()</c>, and <c>Double.parseDouble()</c>.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-wrapper-classes">
      <title>Java primitive types and their wrapper classes</title>
      <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Primitive Type</cell>
          <cell>Wrapper Class</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>byte</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Byte</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>short</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Short</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>int</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Integer</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>long</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Long</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>float</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Float</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>double</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Double</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>char</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Character</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>boolean</c></cell>
          <cell><c>Boolean</c></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Notice that six of the wrapper classes simply capitalize the primitive type name; only <c>int</c> (<c>Integer</c>) and <c>char</c> (<c>Character</c>) capitalize and change the primitive names to full words.
    </p>

    <p>
      Because wrapping and unwrapping objects manually for every operation would be tedious, Java performs these conversions automatically:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Autoboxing is the automatic conversion of a primitive value into its corresponding wrapper object. For example, passing an <c>int</c> literal to an <c>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</c>'s <c>add</c> method automatically boxes the <c>int</c> into an <c>Integer</c> object.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Unboxing is the automatic conversion of a wrapper object back into its primitive value. For example, assigning an element retrieved from an <c>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</c> to an <c>int</c> variable<mdash />or using it in arithmetic<mdash />automatically extracts the primitive <c>int</c>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <program language="java"><![CDATA[ArrayList<Integer> scores = new ArrayList<Integer>();

scores.add(95);               // Autoboxing: int 95 is wrapped in an Integer object
scores.add(88);

int first = scores.get(0);    // Unboxing: Integer object is converted back to int 95
int total = first + scores.get(1);  // scores.get(1) is unboxed before addition]]></program>

    <p>
      While autoboxing and unboxing make wrapper types convenient to use, keep two caveats in mind:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <title>Memory and performance overhead</title>
        <p>
          Autoboxing hides the conversion in your source code, but each element in an <c>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</c> is still a separate object on the heap rather than a raw 32-bit integer. For large numeric datasets, a primitive array (<c>int[]</c>) uses far less memory.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Object behavior (<c>null</c> and <c>==</c>)</title>
        <p>
          Because wrapper variables hold object references, a wrapper reference can be <c>null</c>; attempting to unbox a <c>null</c> wrapper throws a <c>NullPointerException</c>. Likewise, when comparing two wrapper objects directly (such as two <c>Integer</c> variables), use <c>.equals()</c> rather than <c>==</c>, because <c>==</c> compares memory addresses rather than the underlying values.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-7-7-3-arraylist-algorithms">
    <title>ArrayList Algorithms</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-7-7-3-1-averaging">
      <title>Averaging with an Enhanced <c>for</c> Loop</title>

      <p>
        Let’s look at a loop that sums values in an <c>ArrayList</c> and then calculates the average. Compare <xref ref="listing-arraylist-average" /> to the first loop in <xref ref="listing-weekly-aqi-demo" />, which averaged elements in an integer array:
      </p>

      <listing xml:id="listing-arraylist-average">
        <caption>Averaging values in an <c>ArrayList</c>.</caption>
        <program language="java" line-numbers="yes"><![CDATA[import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ProcessReadingsArrayList {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Holds a week of AQI readings.
        ArrayList<Integer> readings = new ArrayList<>();

        // Adds seven readings to the ArrayList.
        readings.add(45);
        readings.add(68);
        readings.add(112);
        readings.add(85);
        readings.add(38);
        readings.add(142);
        readings.add(55);

        // Totals the readings.
        int total = 0;
        for (int reading : readings) {
            total += reading;
        }

        // Displays the average AQI reading for the week.
        System.out.printf("The average AQI is %.1f.%n", (double)total / readings.size());
    }
}]]></program>
      </listing>

      <p>
        On line 7, we declare an <c>ArrayList</c> of type <c>Integer</c> (the wrapper class for primitive integer values) and allocate the <c>ArrayList</c> with the <c>new</c> keyword. We don’t need to specify a size<mdash />the <c>ArrayList</c> begins at size zero (no elements), and will grow as needed.
      </p>

      <p>
        We then add seven values into the <c>ArrayList</c> on lines 10-16 using the <c>add</c> method. Unlike arrays, there is no <q>syntactic sugar</q> for specifying an initial set of values, though in real programs you rarely hard-code a list like this<mdash />you usually read values from a file or the console, or build them as the program runs.
      </p>

      <p>
        Our summing code is on lines 19-22, with <c>total</c> acting as our gatherer variable. Notice that we can use the enhanced <c>for</c> loop with an <c>ArrayList</c>, just as we used it with an array. The <c>reading</c> variable is therefore the next value in the <c>ArrayList</c> (and not an index), so we add it directly to our <c>total</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        On line 25, we calculate the average by casting <c>total</c> to a <c>double</c> and dividing by <c>readings.size()</c><mdash />the number of elements in the <c>ArrayList</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The averaging code does assume that the <c>ArrayList</c> is non-null and non-empty. We allow this here because it’s a demonstration that populates the <c>ArrayList</c> and then immediately averages the values. It is best practice to check the precondition that a collection’s size is not zero before dividing by it, though! To check that an array isn't empty, we use its <c>length</c> field; with an <c>ArrayList</c>, we could check <c>size() &gt; 0</c>, or use the more readable <c>isEmpty()</c> method instead.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-7-7-3-2-most-wanted">
      <title>Passing an ArrayList to a Most-Wanted Method</title>

      <p>
        We can see <c>isEmpty()</c> used in this <c>findHighest</c> method:
      </p>

      <program language="java"><![CDATA[/**
 * Finds the highest value in the ArrayList values.
 *
 * Preconditions:  values != null and !values.isEmpty()
 * Postconditions: Returns the highest value in values.
 *
 * @param values   An ArrayList of integers.
 * @return         The greatest value in values.
 */
public static int findHighest(ArrayList<Integer> values) {
    assert values != null : "values is null";
    assert !values.isEmpty() : "Can't find the highest value in an empty list.";

    // Finds the highest value.
    int highest = values.get(0);
    for (int i = 1; i < values.size(); i++) {
        if (values.get(i) > highest) {
            highest = values.get(i);
        } // if
    } // for
    return highest;
}]]></program>

      <p>
        We have two preconditions on our method: that the <c>ArrayList</c> <c>values</c> is non-null and that it’s not empty. Note that when we want to negate the return value of a method, we use the not (<c>!</c>) operator. We want to ensure that there is at least one value in <c>values</c>, so we write in our precondition and assertion <c>!values.isEmpty()</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice that we don’t need to pass a separate <c>size</c> variable into <c>findHighest</c>, as we do with a partially filled array, because the <c>ArrayList</c> keeps track of the number of valid elements (its size) for us. The only parameter is the <c>ArrayList</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Once we’ve validated our preconditions, we use a standard most-wanted holder algorithm to find the largest value in the <c>ArrayList</c>. The only difference is that we use the <c>ArrayList</c> <c>size</c> and <c>get</c> methods rather than a <c>length</c> field and bracket indexing as we do with an array. We set our most-wanted holder variable <c>highest</c> to the value at index <c>0</c>, and then use a standard <c>for</c> loop to loop from <c>1</c> through <c>values.size() - 1</c>. Within the loop, we use <c>get(i)</c> to access the next element in the <c>ArrayList</c> and compare it to <c>highest</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        We must also use an indexed <c>for</c> loop if we need to change values in an <c>ArrayList</c><mdash />an enhanced <c>for</c> loop only gives us a copy of each value in the loop variable, whereas modifying the list requires passing the element's index to <c>set</c>. For example, this loop modifies each element in an <c>ArrayList</c> of quiz scores, adding five points to each score, with a maximum value of 100. It passes the index to the <c>get</c> method to access the value and then uses the index again to store the new value using the <c>set</c> method.
      </p>

      <program language="java"><![CDATA[// Add 5 bonus points to each score, capped at 100.
for (int i = 0; i < scores.size(); i++) {
    int curved = scores.get(i) + 5;
    if (curved > 100) {
        curved = 100;
    }
    scores.set(i, curved);
}]]></program>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-7-7-3-3-removing-while-iterating">
      <title>Removing Elements While Iterating</title>

      <p>
        You have now seen both ways to traverse an <c>ArrayList</c>: an enhanced <c>for</c> loop when you only need to inspect each element in order, and an index-based <c>for</c> loop when you need the index, start past index <c>0</c>, or want to modify elements via <c>set</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        One operation requires extra care: removing elements while looping through an <c>ArrayList</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, never call <c>remove</c> on an <c>ArrayList</c> inside an enhanced <c>for</c> loop<mdash />Java will immediately throw a <c>ConcurrentModificationException</c> at runtime.
      </p>

      <p>
        Using a standard forward-counting <c>for</c> loop avoids that exception, but it introduces a subtle logic bug. Consider removing every failing score (below <c>60</c>) from <c>[85, 42, 30, 90]</c>:
      </p>

      <program language="java"><![CDATA[// BUGGY: skips consecutive matching elements
for (int i = 0; i < scores.size(); i++) {
    if (scores.get(i) < 60) {
        scores.remove(i);
    }
}]]></program>

      <p>
        When <c>i</c> is <c>1</c>, <c>scores.get(1)</c> is <c>42</c>, so <c>scores.remove(1)</c> removes <c>42</c>. Immediately, <c>30</c> shifts left into index <c>1</c> and <c>90</c> shifts into index <c>2</c>. At the end of the loop body, <c>i++</c> increments <c>i</c> to <c>2</c><mdash />skipping <c>30</c> completely. The loop finishes with <c>[85, 30, 90]</c>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here are two approaches to fix this bug:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <title>Iterate backward (recommended)</title>
          <p>
            Start at <c>scores.size() - 1</c> and count down to <c>0</c>. Removing an element at index <c>i</c> only shifts elements at indices greater than <c>i</c><mdash />elements you have already checked<mdash />so no unchecked element ever shifts past <c>i</c>:
          </p>
          <program language="java"><![CDATA[for (int i = scores.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (scores.get(i) < 60) {
        scores.remove(i);
    }
}]]></program>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Adjust <c>i</c> when removing (or use a <c>while</c> loop)</title>
          <p>
            If you must iterate forward, decrement <c>i</c> immediately after calling <c>scores.remove(i)</c> (or only increment <c>i</c> when you do not remove) so the next iteration re-checks the element that shifted into index <c>i</c>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-7-7-4-tradeoffs">
    <title>Trade-offs: Fixed Arrays vs. ArrayList</title>

    <p>
      Both standard arrays and <c>ArrayList</c> objects store ordered sequences of elements, and each has clear strengths depending on the problem:
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-array-vs-arraylist">
      <title>Comparing fixed arrays and <c>ArrayList</c></title>
      <tabular halign="left" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col width="22%"/>
        <col width="39%"/>
        <col width="39%"/>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Feature</cell>
          <cell>Standard Array (<c>int[]</c>, <c>String[]</c>)</cell>
          <cell><c>ArrayList&lt;T&gt;</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Size</cell>
          <cell>Fixed at creation</cell>
          <cell>Grows and shrinks automatically</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Element types</cell>
          <cell>Primitives and objects</cell>
          <cell>Objects only (primitives require wrapper types)</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Syntax</cell>
          <cell><c>arr[i]</c>, <c>arr[i] = val</c>, <c>arr.length</c></cell>
          <cell><c>list.get(i)</c>, <c>list.set(i, val)</c>, <c>list.size()</c></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Memory &amp; speed</cell>
          <cell>Minimal overhead; fastest for primitives</cell>
          <cell>Extra heap overhead for object wrappers and resizing</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Built-in operations</cell>
          <cell>Manual loops for insert/remove; <c>Arrays.toString()</c></cell>
          <cell>Built-in <c>add</c>, <c>remove</c>, <c>isEmpty()</c>, and <c>toString()</c></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Use a standard array when:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          You know the exact number of elements in advance (such as days of the week or a fixed-size board).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          You are storing large amounts of primitive numeric data where memory usage and speed are critical.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          You are working with multidimensional grids (such as a two-dimensional table or image pixels, covered in Section 7.9) or native C/C++ libraries.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Use an <c>ArrayList</c> when:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          You do not know how many elements you will need to store (such as reading unknown-length user or file input).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Elements will be frequently added, inserted, or removed while the program runs.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          You want built-in methods like <c>add</c>, <c>remove</c>, <c>size</c>, and <c>isEmpty</c> without tracking a separate <c>count</c> variable by hand.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>
</section>
